NAME
    lua - execute Lua 5.5 source code

SYNOPSIS
    lua [OPTIONS] [SCRIPT [ARG...]]
    lua --version

DESCRIPTION
    Runs Lua 5.5.1 source from one or more -e arguments, a filesystem file, or
    standard input. -l MODULE loads a module before the script; -l NAME=MODULE
    also stores its result in global NAME. -E ignores environment-based Lua
    configuration, -W enables warnings, and -v prints the version. Remaining
    arguments are available in the global
    arg table, starting at arg[1]. With no source argument, reads standard
    input.

    The base, package, coroutine, debug, io, math, os, string, table, and utf8
    libraries are present. loadfile, dofile, require, package.preload, and the
    Lua-file module searcher use the declared read-only filesystem capability.
    The default module path searches /share/lua/5.5, /share/lua, and the current
    directory. In-memory and file-backed binary chunks are accepted.

    io provides standard streams, temporary in-memory files, and filesystem
    files, including lines, formatted reads and writes, append/update modes,
    flushing, and seeking. Lua has explicit filesystem-read and
    filesystem-mutation authority. Writable files are buffered in the Lua heap
    and replaced through TROE's streamed mutation service when flushed or closed.
    io.popen and C dynamic modules are unavailable because TROE has no compatible
    pipe or dynamic-loader service.

    os.clock follows Lua semantics and returns process CPU seconds charged while
    the application executes unprivileged code. Kernel services, waiting, and
    descheduled time are excluded. os.time() returns whole Unix seconds from
    TROE's wall clock. os.time(table) and os.date implement the standard calendar
    interface in UTC; TROE currently has no per-process timezone database or
    daylight-saving setting. Wall time is not monotonic and may step when corrected. For an
    end-to-end benchmark long enough to tolerate one-second resolution, use
    os.time() before and after the workload. That elapsed result includes kernel
    service, waiting, and descheduling time. os.exit terminates the Lua
    application with an optional Unix-style low-8-bit command status and cannot
    be caught with pcall. Its second argument
    follows Lua semantics: false (the default) skips Lua-state closing, while
    true closes the state and runs its closing work before termination. TROE
    reclaims the application heap after either form. os.remove deletes regular
    files or symbolic links. os.rename preserves regular-file contents through a
    replace-and-remove operation; it is not yet an atomic provider-level rename.
    KEX invocations do not yet carry a mutable environment. os.getenv exposes
    stable PWD, HOME, PATH, TMPDIR, SHELL, USER, and LOGNAME values and returns
    nil for unknown names. os.execute reports that no synchronous child-process
    service is available. The fixed C locale is the only supported locale.

    Lua uses a bounded 8 MiB TLSF heap. Exhausting the heap raises a normal protected Lua memory error;
    it cannot grow memory or access ambient host resources.

EXAMPLES
    Working example programs are shipped as architecture-independent data:

        lua /share/lua/examples/strings.lua
        lua /share/lua/examples/math.lua
        lua /share/lua/examples/tables-coroutines.lua
        lua /share/lua/examples/compatibility.lua
        lua /share/lua/examples/files.lua

    Measure a sufficiently long end-to-end workload in whole wall seconds:

        local t0 = os.time()
        workload()
        print(os.time() - t0)

    Read them with cat, copy one to /tmp, and adapt it there. The immutable
    originals remain available in the recovery root.
